\title{Holographic Primes: Finite Euler Products,\\Bulk Occupation Sums, and Tropical Vacua}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{17 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a finite and unconditional partition-function model in which local prime modes form a boundary description and their joint occupation profiles form a bulk description. For an arbitrary finite mode set, real local energies, occupation cutoff, and inverse temperature, the product of local geometric partition sums is exactly the Gibbs sum over all occupation profiles. Specializing to primes $p<x$ with energy $\log p$ yields a finite prime holographic factorization. Removing both cutoffs in the region of absolute convergence recovers $\prod_p(1-p^{-\beta})^{-1}=\zeta(\beta)$ for $\beta>1$. Tropicalization selects the vacuum energy zero, and a finite-state bound quantifies the low-temperature limit. We present algorithms and cutoff diagnostics while separating these results from unproved claims about completed duality, zero statistics, and spectral stability.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Euler products compress global arithmetic into local factors. Statistical mechanics performs a parallel compression: a sum of uncoupled local energies produces a factored partition function. We make this common algebra explicit by treating each selected prime as a bosonic mode and each vector of occupation numbers as a bulk configuration.

The term \emph{holographic} has a restricted meaning here. The boundary is the factored collection of local partition sums; the bulk is the expanded occupation lattice. Their equality is exact at finite cutoffs and assumes neither spacetime geometry nor gravitational dynamics. Three levels must remain distinct: finite factorization is algebraic; the infinite prime identity is analytic and valid in its convergence region; tropicalization is order-theoretic minimization. This distinction makes the model both reliable and extensible.

\section{Finite occupation systems}
Let $I$ be a finite set, let $E:I\to\R$ assign local energies, and fix $N\in\N$.

\begin{definition}[Occupation space and Hamiltonian]
The occupation space is
\[
 \Omega_{I,N}=\{a:I\to\{0,1,\ldots,N\}\},
\]
and its cardinality is $(N+1)^{|I|}$. For $a\in\Omega_{I,N}$, define
\[
 H_{E,N}(a)=\sum_{i\in I}a_iE_i.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Bulk and boundary partitions]
For $\beta\in\R$, define
\[
 \Zbulk(E,N,\beta)=\sum_{a\in\Omega_{I,N}}e^{-\beta H_{E,N}(a)}
\]
and
\[
 \Zbdry(E,N,\beta)=\prod_{i\in I}\left(\sum_{n=0}^{N}e^{-\beta nE_i}\right).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Boltzmann factorization]
For every $a\in\Omega_{I,N}$,
\[
 e^{-\beta H_{E,N}(a)}=\prod_{i\in I}e^{-\beta a_iE_i}.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Substitute the Hamiltonian and apply the exponential addition law over the finite sum.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite Holographic Factorization]
For every finite $I$, every $E:I\to\R$, every $N\geq0$, and every $\beta\in\R$,
\[
 \Zbdry(E,N,\beta)=\Zbulk(E,N,\beta).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Expand the product of local sums. Choosing one summand at every mode is equivalent to choosing a function $a\in\Omega_{I,N}$. The corresponding product is $\prod_i e^{-\beta a_iE_i}$, which equals $e^{-\beta H_{E,N}(a)}$ by the lemma. Each profile occurs exactly once.
\end{proof}

The theorem includes $N=0$ and the empty mode set: in either vacuum-only case both sides equal $1$. No positivity is required for this finite identity.

\section{Prime modes and arithmetic states}
For $x\in\N$, let
\[
 P_x=\{p\in\N:p<x\text{ and }p\text{ is prime}\}.
\]

\begin{definition}[Prime energy]
For $p\in P_x$, set $E_p=\log p$. For an occupation profile $a$, set
\[
 H_{x,N}(a)=\sum_{p\in P_x}a_p\log p.
\]
\end{definition}

Every prime is at least $2$, hence every prime energy is nonnegative. The profile $a$ represents
\[
 m(a)=\prod_{p\in P_x}p^{a_p},
\]
so $H_{x,N}(a)=\log m(a)$ and $e^{-\beta H_{x,N}(a)}=m(a)^{-\beta}$. Unique factorization makes this correspondence injective.

\begin{theorem}[Finite Prime Hologram]
For all $x,N\in\N$ and $\beta\in\R$,
\[
 \prod_{p\in P_x}\sum_{n=0}^{N}p^{-\beta n}
 =\sum_{a:P_x\to\{0,\ldots,N\}}
 \exp\left(-\beta\sum_{p\in P_x}a_p\log p\right).
\]
Equivalently, this is $\sum_{m\in S_{x,N}}m^{-\beta}$, where $S_{x,N}$ contains exactly the positive integers whose prime factors lie below $x$ and whose prime exponents are at most $N$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply finite holographic factorization with $I=P_x$ and $E_p=\log p$, then use $e^{-\beta n\log p}=p^{-\beta n}$. The integer description follows from unique factorization.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
For modes $2$ and $3$ and $N=2$,
\[
 Z=(1+2^{-\beta}+2^{-2\beta})(1+3^{-\beta}+3^{-2\beta}).
\]
Its nine bulk states represent $2^a3^b$ with $0\leq a,b\leq2$.
\end{example}

\section{The infinite prime partition}
For fixed $p$ and $\beta>0$,
\[
 \sum_{n=0}^{\infty}p^{-\beta n}=(1-p^{-\beta})^{-1}.
\]
Removing the prime cutoff requires absolute convergence.

\begin{definition}[Infinite prime partition]
For real $\beta>1$, define
\[
 Z_{\mathrm{prime}}(\beta)=\prod_p(1-p^{-\beta})^{-1}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Infinite Prime Partition Identity]
If $\beta>1$, then
\[
 Z_{\mathrm{prime}}(\beta)=\zeta(\beta).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The Dirichlet series $\sum_{n\geq1}n^{-\beta}$ converges absolutely. Expanding the absolutely convergent product of geometric series, unique factorization makes every positive integer occur exactly once.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Logarithmic representation]
For $\beta>1$,
\[
 \exp\left(\sum_p-\log(1-p^{-\beta})\right)=\zeta(\beta).
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Absolute convergence permits summing the logarithms of the positive Euler factors and then exponentiating.
\end{proof}

The hypothesis $\beta>1$ is essential to this argument. Analytic continuation and the archimedean factor in the completed zeta function supply information absent from finite distributivity.

\section{Tropical dequantization}
\begin{definition}[Tropical partition]
For a finite configuration space $\Omega$ and Hamiltonian $H$, define
\[
 \mathcal T(H)=\inf_{a\in\Omega}H(a).
\]
The infimum is a minimum because $\Omega$ is finite.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Energy nonnegativity]
If $E_i\geq0$ for every $i$, then $H_{E,N}(a)\geq0$ for every occupation profile.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Each $a_iE_i$ is nonnegative, as is their sum.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Vacuum energy]
The profile $a^{(0)}_i=0$ has $H_{E,N}(a^{(0)})=0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Every term is $0\cdot E_i$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Tropical Vacuum Theorem]
If all local energies are nonnegative, then
\[
 \mathcal T(H_{E,N})=0.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Energy nonnegativity gives a lower bound of zero, while the vacuum attains zero.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Prime Tropical Vacuum]
For every $x,N\in\N$, $\mathcal T(H_{x,N})=0$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Use $\log p\geq0$ and apply the theorem. The empty prime set is included because its unique empty profile has zero energy.
\end{proof}

If $M=(N+1)^{|I|}$ and $\beta>0$, nonnegative energies imply
\[
 1\leq\Zbulk(E,N,\beta)\leq M.
\]
Therefore
\[
 0\leq\frac{1}{\beta}\log\Zbulk(E,N,\beta)
 \leq\frac{|I|\log(N+1)}{\beta}\longrightarrow0,
\]
quantifying convergence to the tropical vacuum.

\section{Algorithms}
Boundary evaluation computes every local sum and multiplies them. Direct summation costs $O(|I|N)$ arithmetic operations and constant auxiliary storage; geometric closed forms reduce the count to $O(|I|)$ when applicable. Bulk enumeration loops over $(N+1)^{|I|}$ profiles, computes each Hamiltonian, and costs $O(|I|(N+1)^{|I|})$. It is slower but exposes the state space and provides an independent numerical check.

For logarithmic prime energies, tropical ground-state computation takes constant time after verifying nonnegativity: the answer is zero. More generally, separability gives $\sum_i\min(0,NE_i)$ if negative local energies are allowed.

\section{Error structure and finite-size analysis}
For fixed primes and $\beta>0$, put $q_p=p^{-\beta}$. Then
\[
 \sum_{n=0}^{N}q_p^n=\frac{1-q_p^{N+1}}{1-q_p},
 \qquad
 \sum_{n=N+1}^{\infty}q_p^n=\frac{q_p^{N+1}}{1-q_p}.
\]
Consequently,
\[
 Z_{x,N}(\beta)=\prod_{p\in P_x}
 \frac{1-p^{-\beta(N+1)}}{1-p^{-\beta}},
\]
and the exact occupation-cutoff ratio is
\[
 \frac{Z_{x,N}(\beta)}{Z_{x,\infty}(\beta)}
 =\prod_{p\in P_x}(1-p^{-\beta(N+1)}).
\]
For $\beta>1$, the independent prime-tail ratio is
\[
 \frac{\zeta(\beta)}{Z_{x,\infty}(\beta)}
 =\prod_{p\geq x}(1-p^{-\beta})^{-1}.
\]
Its logarithm tends to zero by absolute convergence.

At low temperature, if an excited state exists and $\Delta$ is its least positive energy, then
\[
 0\leq Z_{x,N}(\beta)-1
 \leq\bigl((N+1)^{|P_x|}-1\bigr)e^{-\beta\Delta}.
\]
For a nonempty prime set and $N\geq1$, one may use $\Delta\geq\log2$. This shows exponential concentration on the vacuum.

Useful numerical diagnostics are the residual $|\Zbdry-\Zbulk|$, the tropical proxy $\beta^{-1}\log Z$, the exact occupation ratio, and the finite-prime ratio against $\zeta(\beta)$ for $\beta>1$. They distinguish computational discrepancy from cutoff error.

The order of limits matters. Taking $\beta\to\infty$ first retains only the vacuum, whereas taking $N\to\infty$ first at fixed $x$ and $\beta>0$ produces a finite Euler product. Sending $x\to\infty$ afterward gives the ordinary zeta product only for $\beta>1$. A stable minimum energy does not imply convergence of the total thermal weight.

For $k$ modes, the bulk has $(N+1)^k$ states while the boundary requires only $O(kN)$ local data. This exponential compression depends on additivity. Interaction terms such as $J_{pq}a_pa_q$ prevent one-mode factorization.

After normalization by $Z$, the Gibbs weights define independent occupation variables with
\[
 \mathbb P(a_p=n)=\frac{p^{-\beta n}}{\sum_{j=0}^{N}p^{-\beta j}}.
\]
Hence $\mathbb E[H]=\sum_{p\in P_x}(\log p)\mathbb E[a_p]$. The same independence that proves factorization also limits spectral applications: connected correlations between distinct basic modes vanish. Comparison with correlated zero statistics would require an additional mechanism that creates nontrivial correlations.

\section{Applications and interpretation}
A boundary description with $|I|$ local factors compactly represents a bulk with $(N+1)^{|I|}$ states. This is the same advantage exploited by generating functions and independent-particle models. In the prime case, every state is a bounded prime factorization, the Hamiltonian is the logarithm of its integer, and its Gibbs weight is an inverse power.

Tropically, logarithms turn integer multiplication into energy addition, while cooling turns thermal aggregation into minimization. The resulting vacuum theorem reports only ground energy, not excited spectra or correlations. Interactions between modes would generally destroy the elementary product and demand a richer boundary encoding.

\section{Scope, limitations, and future work}
The established results are finite boundary--bulk equality, its prime specialization, the zeta identity for $\beta>1$, and the zero tropical vacuum with quantitative finite-state convergence. They do not establish the completed functional equation, zero pair correlation, or the Riemann Hypothesis.

Future work should first remove prime and occupation cutoffs jointly with explicit independent error terms. A second task is to represent the archimedean gamma factor as a distinguished sector at infinity; any reflection law $s\mapsto1-s$ must include it. A third direction is a tropical reflection law with controlled finite-temperature defect. A fourth is to compare centered occupation-energy correlations, after explicit smoothing and scaling, with conjectural zero statistics. Finally, a stability interpretation of the critical line requires a defined geometry, perturbation operator, and equivalence theorem connecting its spectrum to zeta zeros.

\section{Conclusion}
A finite product of local partition sums and a finite Gibbs sum over occupation profiles are exact descriptions of one system. Prime energy $\log p$ turns the bulk into bounded prime factorizations and the boundary into a truncated Euler product. In the absolute-convergence region, removing cutoffs recovers $\zeta(\beta)$; tropical dequantization selects vacuum energy zero. The framework is useful precisely because finite algebra, convergent analysis, tropical minimization, and conjectural spectral extensions remain clearly separated.

